  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Turning more humid with a few rounds of showers and \nscattered thunderstorms late this afternoon through Sunday evening.\n\nA southern stream shortwave will move from the Ohio Valley this \nevening northeastward across NY/PA overnight. DPVA ahead of the \nshortwave will combine with isentropic upglide and moisture \ntransport in a general warm advection pattern to support an area of \nshowers crossing the region from southwest to northeast from mid \nafternoon through this evening. The most concentrated area of rain \nwill likely track from the western Southern Tier into the Finger \nLakes and Tug Hill region later this afternoon through this evening, \nwith the Buffalo, Rochester, and Watertown areas all near the \nnorthwest edge of the more widespread rain. Instability will be \nlimited, but may be sufficient to support a few widely scattered \nweak thunderstorms as well. Some heavy downpours will likely cross \nthe Southern Tier and Finger Lakes, but the more organized flood \nrisk will likely remain south of the area across PA.\n\nOvernight, the initial shortwave will move into eastern NY and New \nEngland, with most of the showers ending from west to east. The one \nexception may be across the North Country, where a few light showers \nmay linger through much of the night. \n\nSunday morning will be dry in most areas, with just a few scattered \nlight showers possible across the North Country. A strong northern \nstream trough will advance southeast into the Great Lakes, with a \nsurface cold front approaching the eastern Great Lakes by evening. \nHeight falls will bring increasing large scale ascent near the time \nof peak heating, although instability will only be modest. Expect \nwidely scattered thunderstorms to develop mid afternoon through mid \nevening. The initial focus for convective initiation will be along \nlake breeze boundaries, with the incoming front then possibly \ncapturing convection and moving it southeast across the area during \nthe early to mid evening. Coverage of thunderstorms in the afternoon \nand evening will likely remain widely scattered, with plenty of rain \nfree time.\n\nA few storms may contain gusty winds, with a marginal risk of a few \nisolated severe thunderstorms with localized damaging winds and \nhail. Moderate to strong shear profiles will be more than adequate \nto support severe thunderstorms, but weak instability and poor mid \nlevel lapse rates will be a notable limiting factor.\n\nSunday night, any thunderstorms will end in the evening. A brief \nperiod of upslope and lake effect showers southeast of the lakes may \ndevelop right behind the cold front from late evening through the \nearly overnight, but a rapidly drying synoptic scale airmass will \nbring a quick end to any lake response by Monday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Brief cooldown Monday through early Tuesday morning, \nthen trending warmer again through the middle of next week.\n\nA much cooler airmass will arrive Monday behind the departing cold \nfront, with 850MB temps bottoming out in the +4C to +6C range. Highs \nwill range from the mid to upper 60s for lower elevations to lower \n60s across higher terrain. Northwest flow will support some lake \neffect and upslope clouds in the morning, but strong subsidence and \ndrying will allow for increasing sunshine later in the afternoon.\n\nMonday night will be chilly, with lows dropping into the 40s \nareawide except the immediate lakeshores. Some of the normally \ncooler Southern Tier valleys and Tug Hill region may make a run at \nthe upper 30s.\n\nHigh pressure will drift off the New England coast by Tuesday, with \nsoutherly return flow in its wake allowing for a quick warm-up \nTuesday through midweek. Latest model guidance suggests Tuesday will \nstay mainly dry, with rain chances returning by Wednesday as a \nfrontal boundary moves into the Great Lakes and potentially stalls \nnearby by Thursday.",
